{
  "rules": [
    "Strong positive bias in true prices vs model: model systematically underestimates the level during uptrends (typical miss ~+5 to +8 €/t in days ~5–20). Apply an upward level adjustment when recent momentum is positive.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model reacts too slowly to sharp rallies; early-horizon forecasts (days 1–10) are too flat relative to realized acceleration. Increase forecast slope when the last 5–10 days show consecutive gains or a breakout to new 30d highs.",
    "Hump-shaped error profile: underprediction is largest mid-horizon (roughly days 7–20), then shrinks and can flip to slight overprediction late-horizon as prices mean-revert. Use a horizon-dependent correction: bigger uplift mid-horizon, smaller uplift (or none) beyond ~day 20.",
    "Peak/turn timing error: the model predicts the drawdown too early and too deep after peaks (it starts reverting while realized prices stay elevated longer). Delay mean-reversion in the forecast when volatility spikes upward and spot is near recent highs.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot on declines: once the realized market starts falling, the model remains too high (overestimates) near the end of the 30d window. Add a negative adjustment when the last 3–7 realized days show a clear reversal (lower highs/lower lows)."
  ]
}
